A||||6||WisconsinGT||Strut question||||1
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||11-05-1999||12:10 AM||||I have my 88GT on stands. I am trying to take off the struts. I am able to take off the two nuts on the bottom bolts. But, I cannot even budge the bolts. I even put a jack under the control arm to push the strut up. Hoping to relieve the pressure. But no luck. Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||11-05-1999||12:32 AM||||Get a really big wrench... two, actually.  Hold the bolt head in place while you try to turn the nut.  A breaker bar would be even better.  Soak it with some wd-40 or other penetrating oil... those things can be a bear to get off..||||reg||12
Z||000002||Carrolles||11-05-1999||06:45 AM||||You might try a couple of C-clamps and a short length of flat bar to make a homemade press to push them out.<P>Mine just came right out once I got the nuts loose.<P>Good luck,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||11-05-1999||09:23 AM||||Did you try releasing the three top nuts?  Doing this will cause the whole strut to drop, and allow you to man-handle the strut and wiggle the bolt out.  Use the ol' mallet and drift to knock 'em out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||11-05-1999||11:51 AM||||Thanks for the advice. Do I need to have a jackstand under the strut? I am concerned about any pressure on the strut assembly.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-05-1999||08:35 PM||||I can't remember.... do 88's have rear sway?<P>If they do you need to jack both sides of the cars rear clear of the ground. Other wise the sway will load up.<P>If the bolts are rusted in the holes then screw the nuts back on so they are even with the end of the bolt. put a piece of steel brass or dense plastic on the end of the bolt and give it a good whack. the nut will spread the load and prevent damage to the bolt. Unless your car already has "cam bolts" you likely need new ones anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-05-1999||08:46 PM||||'88s had the rear sway bar on the Formula and GT.  I don't believe the coupes had one.||||reg||1
